WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.279
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Mara Vist and Doll, who's the former China

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:09.750
correspondent for science and also author
of The Scientist and the Spy A True Story

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.730
of China, the FBI and Industrial Espionage.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.340
So great to talk to you today, Mara.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.070
Thanks again for having me on the show.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.400
So let's start with industrial espionage.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.750
Many people not familiar with this or may
have an idea of what it means.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:30.990
Donald Trump notably talking about it typically
in reference to China.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.890
China treating us unfairly, China stealing
intellectual property.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.410
What's the big picture of industrial espionage?

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.269
Is it typically involving in intellectual
property?

00:00:41.269 --> 00:00:45.159
What are the sort of different types that
maybe exist?

00:00:45.159 --> 00:00:52.680
So going after industrial espionage from China
has been a huge priority of the Trump administration.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:59.499
It is one of the ostensible justifications
for the trade war that he started with China.

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:06.590
But the focus on it actually goes back over
a decade to the Obama administration.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:14.280
The FBI made fighting industrial espionage
a top priority and started taking on investigations

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:23.680
involving technologies like the whitener,
Oreo cookies, wind turbines and even genetically

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.170
modified corn, which is at the heart of my
book.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:35.030
So on that note, I mean, so if you think of
the whitener in Oreo cookies, for example,

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:39.540
doesn't sound like there are major national
security implications necessarily.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:45.530
But when it comes to industrial espionage,
how frequently are classified technologies

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:46.530
involved?

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:51.820
How frequently are government contractors
or or programs and research involved?

00:01:51.820 --> 00:02:00.180
There have been cases involving national security
technologies, but the government, the U.S.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:06.640
government has basically been arguing that
an attack on our U.S. corporations and an

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.450
attack on our economic security is an attack
on national security.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:18.360
So there's been this kind of expansive reimagining
of what national security means as this has

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.069
become a priority.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:28.209
When you talk about industrial espionage,
is the motivation typically a motivation of

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:31.739
getting an advantage when it comes to the
ability to earn a profit?

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:35.209
I mean, is that national security instances
aside?

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:42.569
Is that mostly what is at issue here, trying
to earn a profit by getting some kind of technology,

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:46.749
technique, product or otherwise an element
of something from another company?

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:47.749
Sure.

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:48.749
Yeah.

00:02:48.749 --> 00:02:53.739
China is developing very rapidly or it was
developing, I should say, before the start

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:55.920
of the global pandemic.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.810
And there are a lot of companies who are really
just interested in short term getting ahead

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.659
at all costs.

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:06.819
Stealing technology is the most expedient
way to do that.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:14.709
But, you know, I wanted to draw attention
to whether this is the best use of our resources.

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:22.150
The policies that the Trump administration
put in place led to the deterioration of U.S.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.209
China relations, which is now absolutely critical.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:32.610
Now we're seeing with the pandemic that cooperation
could be essential to fighting it.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:38.709
And all of this back and forth over industrial
espionage really led to the decline of of

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:40.109
that relationship.

00:03:40.109 --> 00:03:44.359
So not that an eye for an eye is the right
or the wrong thing, but just so that we have

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:45.529
all the information.

00:03:45.529 --> 00:03:51.389
How frequently is the US doing the same thing
elsewhere?

00:03:51.389 --> 00:04:00.560
The U.S. stance is that we don't spy for the
benefit, specific benefit of our private corporations.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:06.389
There are some people who take issue with
that who point to small examples of it could've

00:04:06.389 --> 00:04:08.090
happened here or there.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:12.379
But, you know, in general, the US is at a
point in our development where we don't need

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:13.659
to.

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:17.549
There were certainly points in our history
where we did take a lot of technologies in

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:18.549
early America.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:22.030
There was quite a lot that was stolen from
England, for example.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:30.331
But China's now at that stage where it is
rapidly developing and where the the US has

00:04:30.331 --> 00:04:32.130
a lot of desirable technologies.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:39.389
I think that the question is, should an ear
of corn be treated like a national security

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:40.389
secret?

00:04:40.389 --> 00:04:41.389
Yes.

00:04:41.389 --> 00:04:42.389
So let's get into that story.

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:47.150
I mean, describe the story that you tell and
highlight in the book, The Scientist and the

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:48.150
Spy.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:55.900
So I look at the case of Robert Mom, who was
a Chinese born engineer who was found in an

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:00.270
Iowa cornfield in 2011.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:06.139
He was initially suspected of trespassing,
but his appearance there ended up setting

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:15.009
off this two year FBI investigation involving
surveillance planes and airport busts and

00:05:15.009 --> 00:05:21.599
the use of a FISA warrant, which is intended
to be reserved for extreme national security

00:05:21.599 --> 00:05:22.800
threats.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.880
And what was he trying to accomplish in that
cornfield?

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.580
Well, it was a Monsanto corn fields.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:37.729
The company treated the next generation high
hybrid corn that was growing there as trade

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:39.330
secrets.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:45.680
Robert Moore worked for a Beijing agricultural
company that wanted to steal that corn.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.930
At one point, they actually tried to send
it back to China in microwave popcorn bags.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:55.099
So they had this kind of harebrained scheme.

00:05:55.099 --> 00:06:01.680
And the FBI, meanwhile, decided to track them
over years and bugging their cars, chasing

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.819
them across the Midwest.

00:06:02.819 --> 00:06:10.500
And so you had this kind of absurd cat and
mouse chase that in the end was a massive

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.990
use of taxpayer taxpayer resources.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:13.990
Yes.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:16.199
So there's there's so much to talk about with
regard to this.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:20.770
I mean, what you mentioned the question of
is this a good use of FBI resources which

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:22.789
are taxpayer funded?

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:24.690
That's one debate to be had.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:31.530
The other question is, is this another example
of corporate power and the degree to which

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:38.300
corporations dictate law enforcement priorities,
whether you want to talk about private prisons,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:44.000
existing influences, the judicial system and
how the judicial system functions?

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.360
Should that be the case?

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.360
I would argue.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.360
No.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.630
Is that not another underlying story here?

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:55.889
Which is that because of what who Monsanto
is, they have a lot of say in what the FBI

00:06:55.889 --> 00:06:56.889
does.

00:06:56.889 --> 00:06:57.889
It doesn't work on.

00:06:57.889 --> 00:06:58.889
Oh, definitely.

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:05.300
You know, so I retraced Robert most steps,
I retrace the steps of the FBI agent who is

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.699
tailing him there.

00:07:06.699 --> 00:07:13.460
At one point there were 70 some agents working
on the case of dozens of people and multiple,

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.680
multiple states.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.160
Just a massive use of resources.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:23.349
And, you know, as I was traveling around the
Midwest talking to farmers about this case,

00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:29.919
there were many people said to me, look, you
know, Monsanto and Dupont pioneer another

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:31.139
company in this.

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:37.180
In this case, they have massively consolidated
over the past few years.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:41.870
They to the point where the price of seed
has gone up for many, many farmers.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:48.219
And when you look at what the impediments
to innovation in agriculture are, that corporate

00:07:48.219 --> 00:07:51.159
consolidation is is a very big one.

00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:56.870
Now, on the other side of this, you know,
it's very easy to say, hey, listen, there's

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:02.099
intellectual property here and the intellectual
property is being violated or threatened.

00:08:02.099 --> 00:08:03.949
You've got a guy standing in a cornfield.

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.159
It's sort of pretty cut and dry.

00:08:05.159 --> 00:08:08.561
Aside from whether the FBI should be involved
there, only local police or whatever, that's

00:08:08.561 --> 00:08:10.130
one question.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:16.050
But on the other side of that is it may be
too easy to get intellectual property protection

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:21.340
for things that one could debate whether they
should or should not be subject to that level

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:26.169
of protection from government, intellectual
property infrastructure in the sense that,

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:31.210
you know, when we talk about what can you
trademark, what can you patent, et cetera.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.709
That's another side to this story.

00:08:34.709 --> 00:08:40.530
That is, you know, I do think there is a big
a big place for IP protection.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:42.200
You know, I'm a writer.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.710
I believe that my my work should be protected.

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.260
I don't want people out there pirating my
book.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.050
Same with my head had absolutely agree.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:57.010
On the other hand, you know, when you look
at what should be done to protect innovation

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:00.720
in this country, there's also a big role for
antitrust enforcement.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:06.620
And that's something that has really taken
a backseat to other priorities in the past

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:07.620
few years.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.120
But even in the Obama years, there were several
big antitrust actions that fell apart.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.760
And one of them involves Monsanto.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:22.600
So even as the Justice Department was bringing
this case on basically on behalf of Monsanto,

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:28.350
there was an antitrust inquiry explored by
another arm of the department that then fell

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.350
apart.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:32.590
Well, that seems to be if you zoom out, that's
the trend, right.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:39.630
Which is when it comes to allowing intellectual
property protection, it by and large benefits

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:42.860
large corporations over small individuals.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:47.850
And we see that, you know, in MySpace, we
see it on platforms like YouTube and others

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.600
where the benefit of the doubt by default
goes to the large media company and not the

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.250
small producer, et cetera.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:59.070
You bring up antitrust, which is an opportunity
to regulate and control some of these large

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.070
corporations.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:04.420
But again, there it's not nearly enough used,
as you're sort of pointing out.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:09.510
So is that the overarching story or what is
really the overarching story here when it

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.060
comes to industrial espionage in your mind?

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.190
Well, ultimately, I was drawn to this story
because it was such a good story, had all

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:23.850
these twists and turns and these interesting
characters, nobody was really for we all good

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.140
or all bad.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.680
And just very kind of complex.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:31.120
And I saw that I could structure it like a
thriller.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:36.389
But at the same time, it also tapped into
all these other themes of corporate control,

00:10:36.389 --> 00:10:43.850
the expansion of what constitutes national
security, of the FBI, turning itself from

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:50.110
a law enforcement agency into a counterintelligence
agency, which has also played a big role in

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:54.670
in the expansion of what constitutes an investigation.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:11:00.600
And and so this by looking at this one small
case, I could get all these other issues.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:04.910
So if we zoom out from this case a little
bit, can you give us some other types of industrial

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:08.310
espionage that maybe are less obvious than
than the case?

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:12.480
We're talking about corn and Monsanto seeds
and some of those others that are under that

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.480
umbrella.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:18.470
What else is included under this umbrella
of industrial espionage more broadly?

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:22.440
Oh, pharmaceuticals.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.470
The insulation that's used in walls.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.250
Any lot of industrial compounds and chemicals.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:37.970
And, you know, basically, if you look at the
major corporations in America and what they're

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:44.940
manufacturing, many of those products have
been the subject of these industrial espionage.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.490
Keep them factions.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:49.410
This stuff can be life and death in some cases.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:50.410
Right.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:54.510
I mean, now when we're talking about a ninety
five masks and masks that are at issue when

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:59.040
it comes to health care workers that are treating
corona virus patients.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:04.930
And, you know, you start hearing about rotten
masks or faulty masks, masks that are called

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:06.720
and ninety five, but aren't really.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.310
I mean, this is all wrapped up with some of
this intellectual property is it not.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.310
Right.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:13.310
Well that yeah.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:14.470
Counterfeiting is a big issue as well.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:17.950
And I've just been looking into that for another
story.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:26.670
Certainly a big concern and that is really
about the concern over PPE and masks and where

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:27.670
they're coming from.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.459
That's really about global supply chain issues.

00:12:30.459 --> 00:12:36.850
And that's certainly been complicated by the
trade war that Trump has started with China.

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:42.610
And, you know, also just by the general decline
in U.S. China relations.

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:49.450
So over the past few weeks, things have really
reached a new low with China at precisely

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:54.790
at the moment when we need we most need to
be cooperating on this issue.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:59.220
So last thing I want to touch on is as far
as the trade war and the inclusion of, again,

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:02.910
trying to stop Chinese spying and theft of
intellectual property.

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:06.040
Donald Trump very loudly said that China's
doing this.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:12.110
He said the trade agreed that not trade agreement,
but the trade, the tariffs and the larger

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:15.530
package that he put in place was going to
solve that.

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:19.730
And he even recently offhandedly said that
this is something that he has now fixed through

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:20.850
his policy.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:25.209
What's the truth of that is that is the policy
that Trump put in place, including tariffs

00:13:25.209 --> 00:13:29.009
actually solving this issue.

00:13:29.009 --> 00:13:30.339
It certainly hasn't.

00:13:30.339 --> 00:13:35.470
It certainly hasn't helped us in terms of
sourcing medical supplies from China because

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:41.769
there are still some tariffs in place, but
just more generally, the.

00:13:41.769 --> 00:13:44.949
He has pushed us away from China.

00:13:44.949 --> 00:13:48.509
Things were already bad when he came into
office.

00:13:48.509 --> 00:13:55.900
But by using phrases like Chinese virus and
then kind of perpetually pointing fingers,

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:59.170
encouraging China to do the same, which they
have.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.310
We've reached a really a new low point.

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:09.430
And in u.s.-china relations, what would be
a policy that would actually work constructively

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:17.019
to resolve the issue of industrial espionage
between the U.S. and China?

00:14:17.019 --> 00:14:25.160
Well, whatever we do, we need to put in place
a policy that doesn't make researchers feel

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:26.220
targeted.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:31.949
So that's another piece of the puzzle, is
that there is this perception among many Chinese,

00:14:31.949 --> 00:14:37.130
American and Chinese scientists they are being
singled out because of their ethnicity and

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.560
some of them maybe have committed infractions.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:45.699
But there are also cases where people have
been arrested who turned out to be innocent.

00:14:45.699 --> 00:14:51.070
And so that's ultimately detrimental to the
U.S. innovation infrastructure.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:56.600
If we drive out a lot of the research force
and we're seeing that now as we try to develop

00:14:56.600 --> 00:15:01.860
vaccines and try to develop treatments for
COBA 19, that many of the people who could

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:09.860
be working on this are now back in China because
they've been driven out by overzealous investigations.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:14.250
We've been speaking with Mara Visting Doll,
who is the former China correspondent for

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:20.149
science and also author of the book The Scientist
and the Spy A True Story of China, the FBI,

00:15:20.149 --> 00:15:21.400
an Industrial Espionage.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:22.970
Mara, so great having you on.

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:25.420
I really appreciate your time.

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:26.139
Thank you again.

